{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775342190-WITDER3QT3ofZrVnZwxK","role":"assistant","created":1775342221,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to implement the Hodrick-Prescott filter myself. The HP filter decomposes a time series y_t into a trend component τ_t and a cyclical component c_t:\n\ny_t = τ_t + c_t\n\nThe trend is found by minimizing:\n∑(y_t - τ_t)^2 + λ∑(Δ²τ_t)²\n\nwhere Δ²τ_t = τ_{t+1} - 2τ_t + τ_{t-1} (second difference)\n\nFor annual data, lambda=100 is the standard smoothing parameter.\n\nLet me implement this.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_518c119c288049ccaadf493d","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Implement Hodrick-Prescott filter from scratch\n\ndef hp_filter(y, lamb=100):\n    \"\"\"\n    Hodrick-Prescott filter\n    y: input series (list or array)\n    lamb: smoothing parameter (default 100 for annual data)\n    \n    Returns: trend component\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(y)\n    \n    # Build the matrix D (second difference matrix)\n    # D is (n-2) x n\n    D = []\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        row = [0] * n\n        row[i] = 1\n        row[i + 1] = -2\n        row[i + 2] = 1\n        D.append(row)\n    D = [[row[j] for row in D] for j in range(n)]  # Transpose to get n x (n-2)\n    D = [[0] * (n - 2) for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        D[i][i] = 1\n        D[i+1][i] = -2\n        D[i+2][i] = 1\n    \n    # Use matrix algebra to solve\n    # trend = (I + lamb*D'D)^(-1) * y\n    # But let's use a simpler approach - iterate to solve\n    \n    # Using the solution via matrix inversion\n    import math\n    \n    # Build the matrix K = I + lamb * D'D\n    DDT = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        for j in range(n - 2):\n            # D'D is (i,j) = sum of products of D[:,i] and D[:,j]\n            pass\n    \n    # Simpler: use sparse matrix approach or iterative method\n    # Let's implement the direct solution using linear equations\n    \n    # For HP filter, the trend satisfies: (I + lamb * D'D) * tau = y\n    # D'D is a tridiagonal matrix\n    \n    # Build D'D (n x n)\n    DDT = [[0.0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    \n    # D has rows: [1, -2, 1, 0, ..., 0] for each i from 0 to n-3\n    # D'D = D^T * D\n    # (D'D)[i][j] = sum_k D[k][i] * D[k][j]\n    \n    # For interior points (i = 1 to n-2):\n    # D has non-zero entries at i, i+1, i+2\n    \n    # Let's build it directly:\n    # For simplicity, let's use the banded structure\n    # D'D is a symmetric tridiagonal matrix with:\n    # diagonal: 1 at corners, 6 in interior\n    # off-diagonal: -4 for first off-diagonal, 1 for second off-diagonal\n    \n    # The (i,j) element of D'D:\n    # For i = 0: [1, -2, 1, 0, ...]\n    #   D'D[0][0] = 1^2 + (-2)^2 = 5\n    #   D'D[0][1] = 1*(-2) + (-2)*1 = -4\n    #   D'D[0][2] = 1*1 = 1\n    \n    # For i = 1: [-2, 1, 0, ...] and [1, -2, 1, ...]\n    # Actually let's compute it properly\n    \n    # Build D matrix explicitly (n-2) x n\n    D_matrix = []\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        row = [0.0] * n\n        row[i] = 1.0\n        row[i + 1] = -2.0\n        row[i + 2] = 1.0\n        D_matrix.append(row)\n    \n    # Compute D'D = D^T * D\n    DDT = [[0.0] * n for _ in range(n)])\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            for k in range(n - 2):\n                DDT[i][j] += D_matrix[k][i] * D_matrix[k][j]\n    \n    # Now build K = I + lamb * D'D\n    K = [[0.0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            K[i][j] = (1.0 if i == j else 0.0) + lamb * DDT[i][j]\n    \n    # Solve K * tau = y using Gaussian elimination\n    # Create augmented matrix\n    A = [K[i][:] + [y[i]] for i in range(n)]\n    \n    # Forward elimination\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Pivot\n        max_row = i\n        for r in range(i + 1, n):\n            if abs(A[r][i]) > abs(A[max_row][i]):\n                max_row = r\n        A[i], A[max_row] = A[max_row], A[i]\n        \n        # Make sure diagonal is non-zero\n        if abs(A[i][i]) < 1e-10:\n            continue\n        \n        # Eliminate\n        for r in range(i + 1, n):\n            factor = A[r][i] / A[i][i]\n            for c in range(i, n + 1):\n                A[r][c] -= factor * A[i][c]\n    \n    # Back substitution\n    tau = [0.0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        if abs(A[i][i]) < 1e-10:\n            tau[i] = y[i]  # fallback\n        else:\n            tau[i] = A[i][n]\n            for c in range(i + 1, n):\n                tau[i] -= A[i][c] * tau[c]\n            tau[i] /= A[i][i]\n    \n    return tau\n\n\n# Test with simple data\ntest_y = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ntau = hp_filter(test_y, lamb=100)\nprint(\"Test:\", test_y)\nprint(\"Trend:\", tau)\n\n# Now with real data\ndata = {\n    2010: (2161728, 3455931),\n    2011: (2302495, 3598086),\n    2012: (2449092, 3538447),\n    2013: (2773979, 3454254),\n    2014: (3020847, 3504199),\n    2015: (3248722, 3687623),\n    2016: (3266689, 3854101),\n    2017: (3314893, 3980720),\n    2018: (3328745, 4107741),\n    2019: (3462195, 4446583),\n    2020: (3419955, 6551871),\n    2021: (4045980, 6818159),\n    2022: (4896119, 6271508),\n    2023: (4439283, 6134433),\n    2024: (4918737, 6751553),\n}\n\nyears = sorted(data.keys())\nreceipts = [float(data[y][0]) for y in years]\noutlays = [float(data[y][1]) for y in years]\n\nprint(\"\\nApplying HP filter with lambda=100...\")\ntrend_receipts = hp_filter(receipts, lamb=100)\ntrend_outlays = hp_filter(outlays, lamb=100)\n\nprint(\"\\nYear | Receipts  | Trend R   | Outlays   | Trend O   | Actual   | Structural\")\nprint(\"-\" * 90)\nfor i, y in enumerate(years):\n    actual = receipts[i] - outlays[i]\n    structural = trend_receipts[i] - trend_outlays[i]\n    print(f\"{y} | {receipts[i]:>9.0f} | {trend_receipts[i]:>9.0f} | {outlays[i]:>9.0f} | {trend_outlays[i]:>9.0f} | {actual:>9.0f} | {structural:>12.0f}